  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/36ea34524b2ca277e8061441492711d36c8065e765d519d85d4008da97c3f8b3.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,N,R$\n\nII. $P,S,T$\n\nIII. $O,P,S$",
  "worked_solution": "- $M,N,R$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/b8fae613772422b14fce2e2db0762f659599f0b17409223ec291802b5d760d9c.png)\n\n- $P,S,T$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/85638c1466360f3e5285c34e84903824140b9e2fc72a0f029ab1ded170b64eb2.png)\n\n- $O,P,S$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/4b7ef30e306e3c7e70dadb1524e1f60a9ea01832215009ef2ea8344cb07e7297.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
